from c8e_base import *
LB(8,1,'Language work: sound words, compound words, if-clauses and questions','Language work for Wit and Wisdom: words, if-clauses, questions and a narrative essay','The riddle in the classroom',
 "In the story, a clever bundle of sticks answered a proud scholar's challenge. Wit also lives in the way we use language: the right word for a sound, a sentence that imagines something that never happened, a question asked with the right tone. This lesson shows the language tools behind the unit.",
 [("Writers use sound words to let you hear a scene. Words like murmur, mumble, thud, groan, rattle, gasped and whispered tell the reader how something sounds. Choose them to match the mood, for example a murmur of excitement spread through the class.","sound words"),
  ("A compound word joins two or more words to make a new meaning. It can be closed, like sunlight or courtroom, hyphenated, like well-planned, or open, like paddy seeds. A dictionary tells you which form is correct.","closed, hyphenated and open compounds"),
  ("Match words to make compounds such as quick-witted, soft-spoken, ill-tempered and common sense, and then use them in sentences to check that they fit.","building compounds"),
  ("An if-clause is the subordinate clause that states a condition; the main clause tells what happens. In 'If you like, I will try my best', the if-clause uses the simple present and the main clause uses will. The condition may or may not be fulfilled, and the sentence is about a possible future.","if-clause and main clause"),
  ("In 'If, by chance, something were to go wrong, our farmers would suffer', the if-clause uses the simple past and the main clause uses would. This imagines a situation that is hypothetical, not real. 'If I were invisible, I would sneak into the secret room' works the same way.","simple past + would"),
  ("In 'If I had known this would happen, I would have chosen another day', the if-clause uses the past perfect and the main clause uses would have and a past participle. It describes what would have been done differently in a past situation that can no longer be changed.","past perfect + would have + past participle"),
  ("Intonation is the rise and fall of your voice. 'Yes or no' questions end with a rising tone, as in 'Are you coming home?'. Question-word questions beginning with what, why, when, how, where or who end with a falling tone, as in 'When will you come home?'.","intonation: rising and falling"),
  ("Keep the word order correct in questions: say 'Who is the cleverest character in the story?', not 'Who the cleverest character is in the story?'.","question word order"),
  ("A narrative essay tells a personal or imagined experience. It has an introduction that sets the context, a body with events in order, with description and the narrator's feelings, and a turning point, and a conclusion that states the outcome and the lesson. It is written in the first person in simple, correct language.","narrative essay: introduction, body, conclusion")],
 ("Complete each sentence correctly and say which kind of if-sentence it is: (a) If it rains tomorrow, we ___ (stay) indoors. (b) If I ___ (be) a bird, I would fly over the hills. (c) If you had listened carefully, you ___ (solve) the puzzle.",
  [("(a) If it rains tomorrow, we will stay indoors. The if-clause is in the simple present and the main clause has will.","This is a possible future, so use simple present + will."),
   ("(b) If I were a bird, I would fly over the hills. The if-clause uses the simple past form were and the main clause uses would.","This imagines something that is not true, so use simple past + would."),
   ("(c) If you had listened carefully, you would have solved the puzzle. The if-clause uses the past perfect and the main clause uses would have + past participle.","The situation is in the past and cannot be changed, so use past perfect + would have + past participle.")]),
 ("Explain the three kinds of if-sentences with one example each, and say how the voice should move in 'Where do you live?' and 'Do you live here?'.",
  ["Possible future: simple present + will","Imaginary: simple past + would","Impossible past: past perfect + would have + past participle","Question-word questions fall; yes-no questions rise"],
  ["Using will in the if-clause itself","Using a rising tone for every question"]),
 ["Sound words, compound words (closed, hyphenated, open) enrich writing.","If-clauses: simple present + will (possible); simple past + would (imaginary); past perfect + would have + past participle (impossible past).","Yes-no questions have a rising tone; question-word questions a falling tone; keep question word order correct.","A narrative essay has an introduction, a body with a turning point and a conclusion with a lesson, in the first person."],
 [mcq("w1-1",1,"Which of these is a hyphenated compound word?","well-planned",[("sunlight","This is a closed compound."),("paddy seeds","This is an open compound.")],"Hyphenated compounds are joined with a hyphen."),
  mcq("w1-2",1,"Which sentence expresses a possible future condition?","If you like, I will try my best.",[("If I were invisible, I would sneak into the secret room.","This imagines something unreal."),("If I had known, I would have chosen another day.","This is about an unchangeable past.")],"Simple present in the if-clause and will in the main clause."),
  mcq("w1-3",1,"Complete: 'If I had known this would happen, I ___ another day.'","would have chosen",[("will choose","This is not about a past condition."),("would choose","The past perfect if-clause needs would have + past participle.")],"Past perfect + would have + past participle."),
  mcq("w1-4",2,"Which question ends with a falling tone?","Where do you live?",[("Is this your cat?","Yes-no questions rise."),("Can I meet your parents?","Yes-no questions rise.")],"Question-word questions fall."),
  mcq("w1-5",2,"Which sound word best fits: 'A ___ of excitement spread through the classroom'?","murmur",[("thud","A thud is a dull falling sound."),("rattle","A rattle is a quick shaking sound.")],"A murmur is a low continuous sound of many voices."),
  tf("w1-6",2,"'Who the cleverest character is in the story?' has correct word order.",False,"The correct order is 'Who is the cleverest character in the story?'."),
  short("w1-7",3,"Complete: If it rains tomorrow, we ___ stay indoors. (one word)",["will","'ll"],"A possible future uses simple present + will."),
  mcq("w1-8",3,"A narrative essay should end with","an outcome and a lesson or message",[("a list of facts","A narrative tells an experience."),("a new character","The conclusion closes the story.")],"The conclusion states the outcome and the lesson."),
  mcq("w1-9",3,"Which sentence is about an imaginary situation?","If I were a magician, I would make books fly.",[("If it rains tomorrow, we will stay in.","This is a possible future."),("If you finish your homework, we will go out.","This is a possible future.")],"Simple past + would shows an imaginary condition.")])
